---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import BarSet from '../components/BarSet.astro';
import StatGrid from '../components/StatGrid.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import { ENGINES, ENGINES_CAVEAT, ECONOMY_RANKS, VALUE_PER_ACRE, RECIRCULATION, IMPORT_GAPS } from '../data/pages.js';
---

<Base
  title="Economy — Oklahoma Inc."
  description="A $213.5 billion economy, its engine room, where its land value concentrates, and what it buys but does not make."
>
  <section class="panel panel--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kick">The economy</p>
      <h1 class="mega">A $213.5 billion<br />machine.</h1>
      <p class="under">
        Step outside the capitol and the bigger income statement is the whole economy.
        It grew 1.5% in real terms in 2025, and its engine room has diversified well
        beyond the derrick.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kick">The engines</p>
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">Oil still leads.<br />It no longer<br />stands alone.</h2>

      <div class="pad">
        <BarSet
          caption="Headline output and footprint by sector"
          rows={ENGINES}
          source="BEA; OERB (oil and gas, FY2024); Oklahoma Department of Commerce (aerospace); United for Oklahoma (tribal); USDA (agriculture)."
          scale={60.3}
        />
      </div>

      <p class="source pad-sm">{ENGINES_CAVEAT}</p>

      <div class="pad"><StatGrid stats={ECONOMY_RANKS} /></div>

      <div class="prose pad">
        <p>
          The quiet story here is <strong>energy convergence</strong>. The same windy
          plains that made Oklahoma the sixth oil state now make it third in wind, and
          almost all of its electricity-generation growth since 2010 has been wind.
          Tinker Air Force Base seeded an aerospace cluster now worth $44 billion and
          206,000 jobs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Concentration cuts both ways. Oil and gas is still about 23% of all economic
          activity and the swing factor in state tax receipts. When crude rolls over,
          gross-production tax can fall half a billion dollars in a single year — which is
          exactly what happened in FY2024.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ctarow"><Cta href="/people" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kick">Value per acre</p>
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">An acre is an acre<br />until you measure<br />what it earns.</h2>
      <p class="under">
        Every one of Oklahoma's 77 counties, measured by assessed property value divided
        by land area. The range runs from $57 an acre in Cimarron, out in the panhandle,
        to $24,831 an acre in Oklahoma County — a 436-fold spread.
      </p>

      <div class="tablewrap pad">
        <table class="vpa">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Assessed value and value per acre, selected counties</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">County</th><th scope="col" class="num">Assessed value</th><th scope="col" class="num">Value per acre</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {VALUE_PER_ACRE.map((r) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{r[0]}</th><td class="num">{r[1]}</td><td class="num">{r[2]}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose pad">
        <p>
          Oklahoma and Tulsa counties together hold roughly <strong>38% of the entire
          state property tax base on about 3% of its land</strong>. Infrastructure —
          roads, pipes, utilities — is built and maintained across the whole map, while
          the value that funds it concentrates in a few points. Every mile of new
          infrastructure is a long-term maintenance liability for the full tax base.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="source pad-sm">
        Value is 2024 gross assessed valuation (Oklahoma Tax Commission, Ad Valorem
        Division) divided by county land area.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--deep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kick kick--deep">The local density principle</p>
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">Not all spending<br />is equal.</h2>
      <p class="under">
        When a dollar is spent at a locally-owned business, a measurably larger share
        recirculates within the community before it leaves. This is a documented
        economic effect, not an argument.
      </p>

      <div class="pad">
        <BarSet
          caption="Estimated share of revenue that recirculates locally before leaving the state"
          rows={RECIRCULATION}
          source='Institute for Local Self-Reliance, "The Economics of Local"; American Independent Business Alliance. Multiplier figures are ranges rather than point estimates.'
          scale={100}
          deep={true}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="ctarow"><Cta href="/atlas" variant="deep" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kick">Documented import gaps</p>
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">What Oklahoma buys<br />but does not make.</h2>

      <ul class="gaps pad">
        {IMPORT_GAPS.map((g) => (
          <li class="gap reveal">
            <h3 class="gap__title">{g[0]}</h3>
            <p class="gap__body">{g[1]}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <p class="source pad-sm">
        Sources: Census trade data; OMES procurement records. Here is demand, and here is
        the local supply gap. Nothing more is claimed.
      </p>

      <div class="ctarow"><Cta href="/work" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tablewrap { overflow-x: auto; }

  .vpa {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 32rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
  }
  .vpa :is(th, td) {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-4) var(--s-3) 0;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .vpa thead th {
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .vpa tbody th { font-weight: 500; }
  .vpa .num {
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .gaps {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(20rem, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .gap { background: var(--surface); padding: var(--s-5); }
  .gap__title { font-size: var(--t-body); font-weight: 600; margin-block-end: var(--s-2); }
  .gap__body { font-size: var(--t-small); color: var(--ink-2); }

  .visually-hidden {
    position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px;
    padding: 0; margin: -1px; overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
